---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { tracks, roleTracks } from '../data/curriculum.ts';

const lessons = await getCollection('lessons');
const guides = await getCollection('guides');
---
<Layout
  title="About Oddversity"
  description="What Oddversity is, who it's for, how it's built, and what it deliberately doesn't do. A free, open AI engineering curriculum with no paywall and no account requirement."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'About', url: '/about' }]}
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header>
      <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
      <h1>A free, complete curriculum for building with AI.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Oddversity is {lessons.length} lessons, {guides.length} guides, and a set of quizzes,
        scenarios and interview questions covering the path from your first prompt to running
        agents in production. All of it is open, none of it is gated, and you never need an
        account to read a page.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The name</h2>
      <p>
        A university it isn't. There is no accreditation here, no admission, no
        fee and no certificate — that last one deliberately, and there's
        <a href="/blog/why-there-is-no-certificate">a whole page on why</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The odd half is the more honest one. Most courses teach the path where
        everything works, then leave you at the edge of it. The material here is
        organised the other way round, around the cases that don't fit: the
        table that lost its headers during chunking, the agent that goes quiet
        at 3am because a token expired and nothing refreshed it, the eval score
        that climbed while quality fell. Those are the odd ones, and they are
        where the learning actually is.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it's for</h2>
      <p>
        Someone who can already get a model to do something interesting in a chat window and
        now wants to know what happens when that becomes a feature with real users, real data,
        real permissions and a real budget. That gap — between the demo and the system — is
        where almost all the difficulty lives, and it's where most material stops.
      </p>
      <p>
        It's also for people arriving from a role rather than a syllabus. There are {roleTracks.length}
        role paths that pick the relevant lessons for one job and skip the rest.
      </p>

      <h2>How it's organised</h2>
      <p>
        {tracks.length} technical tracks, sequenced by dependency rather than by topic
        popularity. A page tells you what it assumes and what it unlocks, so you can start in
        the middle if you already know the start. Alongside the concept lessons there are four
        other page types that do different jobs: worked examples that push one concrete input
        to one concrete output, comparisons that put every named approach side by side,
        cheatsheets for looking things up mid-build, and common-mistakes pages that state the
        plausible-but-wrong model before correcting it.
      </p>

      <h2>What it tries to be</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Complete.</strong> A path, not a pile of disconnected posts. If a lesson
        depends on an idea, that idea has its own page and a link to it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Hands-on.</strong> Runnable code rather than pseudocode, and the boring
        operational details — timeouts, retries, permissions, cost — that decide whether
        something survives contact with users.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Honest about limits.</strong> Every technique gets its failure modes, not just
        its happy path. Where the evidence is thin or contested, the page says so rather than
        rounding to a confident claim. There are no invented statistics here; where a number
        would need a source we don't have, the number isn't used.
      </p>

      <h2>What it deliberately isn't</h2>
      <p>
        It isn't a certification business, and completing something here doesn't produce a
        credential anyone should treat as one. It isn't a news feed — model releases are
        covered where they change how you should build, not as announcements. And it isn't
        vendor-aligned: where providers differ, the difference is the content.
      </p>

      <h2>How it's built</h2>
      <p>
        Astro and MDX, statically rendered, no client-side framework. Content lives as plain
        Markdown files in a repository, which means the whole curriculum is greppable,
        diffable and reviewable. The site ships almost no JavaScript — search, code copying and
        local progress tracking are the whole of it, and everything still works without them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Progress is stored in your browser, not on a server. There's no account, so there's
        nothing to sign into and nothing of yours being kept.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Technical material goes stale, and some of it was wrong to begin with. If you find an
        error, the useful report names the page and the specific claim. Corrections are the
        highest-value contribution anyone can make here.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cta">
      <a href="/learn" class="btn btn-primary">Browse the curriculum</a>
      <a href="/guides" class="btn btn-ghost">Start with a guide</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .wrap { max-width: 760px; margin-inline: auto; padding: 56px 32px 8px; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(30px, 4.6vw, 44px); letter-spacing: -.022em; margin-top: 14px; }
  .lede { margin-top: 20px; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--muted); }
  .prose { margin-top: 36px; }
  .cta { margin-top: 44px; display: flex; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .wrap { padding: 36px 20px 8px; } }
</style>
